WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.709
This is the kilogram. Not a kilogram, the
kilogram.

00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:06.290
The British National Standard Kilogram,

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.950
at the National Physical Laboratory in Teddington.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.929
It's a lump of platinum-iridium alloy,

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:13.799
it's kept in a very very secure safe just
under here,

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:17.770
and it weighs... well, one kilogram.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.800
Mass measurement around the world needs to
be equivalent.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.600
So if I buy something in America that's a
milligram, or a kilogram, or 100 tonnes,

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.490
I need to know that it's equivalent to a milligram,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.770
or a kilogram, or 100 tonnes in the UK.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:32.769
The real key areas of interest, where the
uncertainties are very important,

00:00:32.769 --> 00:00:34.640
are, for example, the pharmaceutical industry

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.519
where you're dealing with very, very small
quantities of active ingredients

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:39.679
and you have to measure them very, very accurately.

00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:42.350
So when, say, your bathroom scales were manufactured,

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:46.489
they were tested against equipment that was
tested against equipment that,

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:49.219
several chains down the line later,

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:51.929
was tested against something like this.

00:00:51.929 --> 00:00:57.269
And this was tested against the International
Prototype Kilogram in Paris.

00:00:57.269 --> 00:01:00.569
And this is a really weird thing to wrap your
head around,

00:01:00.569 --> 00:01:05.160
but that one in Paris? It always weighs a
kilogram.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.570
It doesn't matter if it's gained a tiny amount
of mass from adsorbing air contaminants,

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.470
or lost a tiny amount when it was cleaned.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.030
It is one kilogram.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.950
We know that the International Prototype is
probably not stable,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:21.320
because all its copies are unstable relative
to itself.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.450
But there's nothing above the International
Prototype that you can compare it with

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.270
to tell whether it's changing or not.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.790
Defining international constants by a physical
thing is... not ideal,

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.780
so in the next few years, scientists round
the world

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:37.060
are deciding what the 21st century standard
should be.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.980
The formal definition will be based on physics,
likely Planck's Constant,

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.640
but how do you make that into a physical thing?

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.140
There are two options: first, a fixed number
of atoms.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.460
You can manufacture 1kg sphere of almost-pure,

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:56.430
single-crystal silicon relatively easily.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.490
What you have to measure here is the lattice
spacing,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.880
ie how far the atoms are apart in the sphere,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.230
and the volume of the sphere. And both of
those

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.320
are measurements you can make at a level of
uncertainty

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.729
where you can generate a sphere which has
an accuracy

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.069
of a part in 10-to-the-8, roughly.

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:15.689
The downside of that is it costs you €2.5million
for a sphere.

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:17.870
Finding the kilogram that way seems to make
sense.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.230
We're measuring mass, after all.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:22.349
But there is an alternative which could be
cheaper and easier,

00:02:22.349 --> 00:02:24.689
although it isn't quite as obvious.

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:28.620
The watt balance is an electrical way of measuring
mass.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.650
This is a way of demonstrating simply how
a watt balance works.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.639
We have a loudspeaker here

00:02:35.639 --> 00:02:39.700
and if I pass a current through the coil of
the loudspeaker,

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.999
you can see that the cone will move up and
move down.

00:02:43.999 --> 00:02:47.609
If I was to put a mass on here,

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:51.370
if I restore the position of the mass,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.480
the current I need to do that is a measure
of the weight of that mass.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.310
The problem I've got is that I don't know
the strength of the field of the magnet,

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.109
or the number of turns on the coil.

00:03:02.109 --> 00:03:08.260
And I can do a second experiment by moving
the cone of the speaker, like that.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.230
If I was to measure the velocity with which
I was moving that,

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.849
and also measure the voltage which was produced,

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:20.549
that would tell me that quantity and allow
me to eliminate it from the two experiments.

00:03:20.549 --> 00:03:25.169
Which, if done accurately, is enough to work
out the weight of that mass.

00:03:25.169 --> 00:03:26.719
Whichever approach gets chosen,

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:29.620
there will still be physical reference kilograms
in the world.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.790
They're not going away. But for the first
time,

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.700
we're going to have something absolute to
compare them against.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.499
Thank you to everyone at the National Physical
Laboratory!

00:03:38.499 --> 00:03:40.810
They have a YouTube channel that you can check
out,

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.810
and they occasionally hold open days. Details
are on their web site.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.540
